.Sh DESCRIPTION
This documentation is primarily aimed at provider authors.
See
.Xr provider 7
for further information.
.Pp
The signature (OSSL_OP_SIGNATURE) operation enables providers to
implement signature algorithms and make them available to applications
via the API functions
.Xr EVP_PKEY_sign 3 ,
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify 3 ,
and
.Xr EVP_PKEY_verify_recover 3
(as well as other related functions).
.Pp
All "functions" mentioned here are passed as function pointers between
.Pa libcrypto
and the provider in
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays via
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
arrays that are returned by the provider's
.Xr provider_query_operation 3
function (see
.Xr provider-base 7 Ns ).
.Pp
All these "functions" have a corresponding function type definition
named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name}_fn ,
and a helper function to retrieve the function pointer from an
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
element named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name} .
For example, the "function"
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx
has these:
.Bd -literal
 typedef void *(OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx_fn)(void *provctx, const char *propq);
 static ossl_inline OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx_fn
     OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx(const OSSL_DISPATCH *opf);
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays are indexed by numbers that are provided as macros in
.Xr openssl-core_dispatch.h 7 ,
as follows:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx                 OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_NEWCTX
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_freectx                OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_FREECTX
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_dupctx                 OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DUPCTX

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_query_key_types        OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_QUERY_KEY_TYPES

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_init              OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SIGN_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign                   OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SIGN
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_init      OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SIGN_MESSAGE_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_update    OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SIGN_MESSAGE_UPDATE
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_final     OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SIGN_MESSAGE_FINAL

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_init            OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify                 OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_init    OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY_MESSAGE_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_update  OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY_MESSAGE_UPDATE
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_final   OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY_MESSAGE_FINAL

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_recover_init    OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY_RECOVER_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_recover         OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_VERIFY_RECOVER

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_init       OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_SIGN_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_update     OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_SIGN_UPDATE
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_final      OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_SIGN_FINAL
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign            OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_SIGN

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_init     OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_VERIFY_INIT
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_update   OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_VERIFY_UPDATE
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_final    OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_VERIFY_FINAL
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify          OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_DIGEST_VERIFY

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_ctx_params         OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_GET_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_ctx_params    OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_GETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params         OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SET_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_ctx_params    OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS

 OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_ctx_md_params      OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_GET_CTX_MD_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_ctx_md_params OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_GETTABLE_CTX_MD_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_md_params      OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SET_CTX_MD_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_ctx_md_params OSSL_FUNC_SIGNATURE_SETTABLE_CTX_MD_PARAMS
.Ed
.Pp
A signature algorithm implementation may not implement all of these
functions.
In order to be a consistent set of functions we must have at least a set
of context functions (OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx and
OSSL_FUNC_signature_freectx) as well as a set of "signature" functions,
i.e.
at least one of:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_init, OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_update and OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_final
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_init, OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_update and OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_final
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_recover_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_recover
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_init, OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_update and OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_final
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_init, OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_update and OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_final
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign
.It OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_init and OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify
.El
The
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_ctx_params
functions are optional, but if one of them is provided then the other
one must also be provided.
The same applies to the
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_ctx_params
functions, as well as the "md_params" functions.
.Pp
The
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_dupctx
function is optional.
It is not yet used by OpenSSL.
.Pp
The
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_query_key_types 3
function is optional.
When present, it should return a NULL-terminated array of strings
indicating the key types supported by the provider for signature
operations.
Otherwise the signature algorithm name must match the given key or match
the default signature algorithm name of the key, both checked using
.Xr EVP_SIGNATURE_is_a 3 .
.Pp
A signature algorithm must also implement some mechanism for generating,
loading or importing keys via the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT)
operation.
See
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7
for further details.
.Ss Context Management Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx
should create and return a pointer to a provider side structure for
holding context information during a signature operation.
A pointer to this context will be passed back in a number of the other
signature operation function calls.
The parameter
.Fa provctx
is the provider context generated during provider initialisation (see
.Xr provider 7 Ns ).
The
.Fa propq
parameter is a property query string that may be (optionally) used by
the provider during any "fetches" that it may perform (if it performs
any).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_freectx
is passed a pointer to the provider side signature context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
This function should free any resources associated with that context.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_dupctx
should duplicate the provider side signature context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter and return the duplicate copy.
.Ss Signing Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_init
initialises a context for signing given a provider side signature
context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign
performs the actual signing itself.
A previously initialised signature context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The data to be signed is pointed to be the
.Fa tbs
parameter which is
.Fa tbslen
bytes long.
Unless
.Fa sig
is NULL, the signature should be written to the location pointed to by
the
.Fa sig
parameter and it should not exceed
.Fa sigsize
bytes in length.
The length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
If
.Fa sig
is NULL then the maximum length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
.Ss Message Signing Functions
These functions are suitable for providers that implement algorithms
that accumulate a full message and sign the result of that accumulation,
such as RSA-SHA256.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_init
initialises a context for signing a message given a provider side
signature context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_update
gathers the data pointed at by
.Fa in ,
which is
.Fa inlen
bytes long.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_final
performs the actual signing on the data that was gathered with
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_update .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign
can be used for one-shot signature calls.
In that case,
.Fa tbs
is expected to be the whole message to be signed,
.Fa tbslen
bytes long.
.Pp
For both
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_message_final
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign ,
if
.Fa sig
is not NULL, the signature should be written to the location pointed to
by
.Fa sig ,
and it should not exceed
.Fa sigsize
bytes in length.
The length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
If
.Fa sig
is NULL then the maximum length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
.Ss Verify Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_init
initialises a context for verifying a signature given a provider side
signature context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify
performs the actual verification itself.
A previously initialised signature context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The data that the signature covers is pointed to be the
.Fa tbs
parameter which is
.Fa tbslen
bytes long.
The signature is pointed to by the
.Fa sig
parameter which is
.Fa siglen
bytes long.
.Ss Message Verify Functions
These functions are suitable for providers that implement algorithms
that accumulate a full message and verify a signature on the result of
that accumulation, such as RSA-SHA256.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_init
initialises a context for verifying a signature on a message given a
provider side signature context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_update
gathers the data pointed at by
.Fa in ,
which is
.Fa inlen
bytes long.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_final
performs the actual verification on the data that was gathered with
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_update .
The signature itself must have been passed through the "signature"
.Pf ( Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_SIGNATURE )
.Sx Signature parameters
before this function is called.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify
can be used for one-shot verification calls.
In that case,
.Fa tbs
is expected to be the whole message to be verified on,
.Fa tbslen
bytes long.
.Ss Verify Recover Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_recover_init
initialises a context for recovering the signed data given a provider
side signature context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_recover
performs the actual verify recover itself.
A previously initialised signature context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The signature is pointed to by the
.Fa sig
parameter which is
.Fa siglen
bytes long.
Unless
.Fa rout
is NULL, the recovered data should be written to the location pointed to
by
.Fa rout
which should not exceed
.Fa routsize
bytes in length.
The length of the recovered data should be written to
.Em *routlen .
If
.Fa rout
is NULL then the maximum size of the output buffer is written to the
.Fa routlen
parameter.
.Ss Digest Sign Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_init
initialises a context for signing given a provider side signature
context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to using
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_md_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
The name of the digest to be used will be in the
.Fa mdname
parameter.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_update
provides data to be signed in the
.Fa data
parameter which should be of length
.Fa datalen .
A previously initialised signature context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
This function may be called multiple times to cumulatively add data to
be signed.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_final
finalises a signature operation previously started through
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_init
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_update
calls.
Once finalised no more data will be added through
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_update .
A previously initialised signature context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
Unless
.Fa sig
is NULL, the signature should be written to the location pointed to by
the
.Fa sig
parameter and it should not exceed
.Fa sigsize
bytes in length.
The length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
If
.Fa sig
is NULL then the maximum length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign
implements a "one shot" digest sign operation previously started through
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_sign_init .
A previously initialised signature context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The data to be signed is in
.Fa tbs
which should be
.Fa tbslen
bytes long.
Unless
.Fa sig
is NULL, the signature should be written to the location pointed to by
the
.Fa sig
parameter and it should not exceed
.Fa sigsize
bytes in length.
The length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
If
.Fa sig
is NULL then the maximum length of the signature should be written to
.Em *siglen .
.Ss Digest Verify Functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_init
initialises a context for verifying given a provider side verification
context in the
.Fa ctx
parameter, and a pointer to a provider key object in the
.Fa provkey
parameter.
The
.Em params ,
if not NULL, should be set on the context in a manner similar to
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_md_params .
The key object should have been previously generated, loaded or imported
into the provider using the key management (OSSL_OP_KEYMGMT) operation
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 Ns ).
The name of the digest to be used will be in the
.Fa mdname
parameter.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_update
provides data to be verified in the
.Fa data
parameter which should be of length
.Fa datalen .
A previously initialised verification context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
This function may be called multiple times to cumulatively add data to
be verified.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_final
finalises a verification operation previously started through
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_init
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_update
calls.
Once finalised no more data will be added through
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_update .
A previously initialised verification context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The signature to be verified is in
.Fa sig
which is
.Fa siglen
bytes long.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify
implements a "one shot" digest verify operation previously started
through
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_digest_verify_init .
A previously initialised verification context is passed in the
.Fa ctx
parameter.
The data to be verified is in
.Fa tbs
which should be
.Fa tbslen
bytes long.
The signature to be verified is in
.Fa sig
which is
.Fa siglen
bytes long.
.Ss Signature parameters
See
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
for further details on the parameters structure used by the
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params
functions.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_ctx_params
gets signature parameters associated with the given provider side
signature context
.Fa ctx
and stored them in
.Em params .
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params
sets the signature parameters associated with the given provider side
signature context
.Fa ctx
to
.Em params .
Any parameter settings are additional to any that were previously set.
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
Common parameters currently recognised by built-in signature algorithms
are as follows.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqdigest" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_DIGEST Ns ) <UTF8 string>
Get or sets the name of the digest algorithm used for the input to the
signature functions.
It is required in order to calculate the "algorithm-id".
.It \(dqproperties" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_PROPERTIES Ns ) <UTF8 string>
Sets the name of the property query associated with the "digest"
algorithm.
NULL is used if this optional value is not set.
.El
.Pp
Note that when implementing a signature algorithm that gathers a full
message, like RSA-SHA256, the "digest" and "properties" parameters
should not be used.
For such implementations, it's acceptable to simply ignore them if they
happen to be passed in a call to
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params .
For such implementations, however, it is not acceptable to have them in
the
.Vt OSSL_PARAM
array that's returned by
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_ctx_params .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqsignature" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_SIGNATURE Ns ) <octet string>
Sets the signature to verify, specifically when
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_message_final
is used.
.It \(dqdigest-size" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_DIGEST_SIZE Ns ) <unsigned integer>
Gets or sets the output size of the digest algorithm used for the input
to the signature functions.
The length of the "digest-size" parameter should not exceed that of a
.Vt size_t .
.It \(dqalgorithm-id" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_ALGORITHM_ID Ns ) <octet string>
Gets the DER-encoded AlgorithmIdentifier for the signature operation.
This typically corresponds to the combination of a digest algorithm with
a purely asymmetric signature algorithm, such as SHA256WithECDSA.
.Pp
The
.Xr ASN1_item_sign_ctx 3
function relies on this operation and is used by many other functions
that sign ASN.1 structures such as X.509 certificates, certificate
requests, and CRLs, as well as OCSP, CMP, and CMS messages.
.It \(dqnonce-type" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_NONCE_TYPE Ns ) <unsigned integer>
Set this to 1 to use deterministic digital signature generation with
ECDSA or DSA, as defined in RFC 6979 (see Section 3.2 "Generation of
k"). In this case, the "digest" parameter must be explicitly set
(otherwise, deterministic nonce generation will fail).
Before using deterministic digital signature generation, please read RFC
6979 Section 4 "Security Considerations".
The default value for "nonce-type" is 0 and results in a random value
being used for the nonce
.Sy k
as defined in FIPS 186-4 Section 6.3 "Secret Number Generation".
.Pp
The FIPS provider does not support deterministic digital signature
generation.
.It \(dqkat" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_KAT Ns ) <unsigned integer>
Sets a flag to modify the sign operation to return an error if the
initial calculated signature is invalid.
In the normal mode of operation - new random values are chosen until the
signature operation succeeds.
By default it retries until a signature is calculated.
Setting the value to 0 causes the sign operation to retry, otherwise the
sign operation is only tried once and returns whether or not it was
successful.
Known answer tests can be performed if the random generator is
overridden to supply known values that either pass or fail.
.El
.Pp
The following parameters are used by the OpenSSL FIPS provider:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqfips-indicator" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_FIPS_APPROVED_INDICATOR Ns ) <integer>
A getter that returns 1 if the operation is FIPS approved, or 0
otherwise.
This may be used after calling either the sign or verify final
functions.
It may return 0 if either the "digest-check", "key-check", or
"sign-check" are set to 0.
.It \(dqverify-message" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_FIPS_VERIFY_MESSAGE No <integer>
A getter that returns 1 if a signature verification operation acted on a
raw message, or 0 if it verified a predigested message.
A value of 0 indicates likely non-approved usage of the FIPS provider.
This flag is set when any signature verification initialisation function
is called.
It is also set to 1 when any signing operation is performed to signify
compliance.
See FIPS 140-3 IG 2.4.B for further information.
.It \(dqkey-check" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_FIPS_KEY_CHECK Ns ) <integer>
If required this parameter should be set early via an init function
(e.g.
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_sign_init
or
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_verify_init ) .
The default value of 1 causes an error during the init if the key is not
FIPS approved (e.g.
The key has a security strength of less than 112 bits).
Setting this to 0 will ignore the error and set the approved "indicator"
to 0. This option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes the approved
"fips-indicator" to return 0.
.It \(dqdigest-check" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_FIPS_DIGEST_CHECK Ns ) <integer>
If required this parameter should be set before the signature digest is
set.
The default value of 1 causes an error when the digest is set if the
digest is not FIPS approved (e.g.
SHA1 is used for signing).
Setting this to 0 will ignore the error and set the approved
"fips-indicator" to 0. This option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes
the approved "fips-indicator" to return 0.
.It \(dqsign-check" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_FIPS_SIGN_CHECK Ns ) <integer>
If required this parameter should be set early via an init function.
The default value of 1 causes an error when a signing algorithm is used.
(This is triggered by deprecated signing algorithms).
Setting this to 0 will ignore the error and set the approved
"fips-indicator" to 0. This option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes
the approved "fips-indicator" to return 0.
.It \(dqsign-x931-pad-check" ( Ns Sy OSSL_SIGNATURE_PARAM_FIPS_SIGN_X931_PAD_CHECK Ns ) <integer>
If required this parameter should be set before the padding mode is set.
The default value of 1 causes an error if the padding mode is set to
X9.31 padding for a RSA signing operation.
Setting this to 0 will ignore the error and set the approved
"fips-indicator" to 0. This option breaks FIPS compliance if it causes
the approved "fips-indicator" to return 0.
.El
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_ctx_params
get a constant
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array that describes the gettable and settable parameters, i.e.
parameters that can be used with
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ctx_params
respectively.
.Ss MD parameters
See
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
for further details on the parameters structure used by the
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_md_ctx_params 3
and
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_md_ctx_params 3
functions.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_md_ctx_params 3
gets digest parameters associated with the given provider side digest
signature context
.Fa ctx
and stores them in
.Em params .
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_ms_ctx_params 3
sets the digest parameters associated with the given provider side
digest signature context
.Fa ctx
to
.Em params .
Any parameter settings are additional to any that were previously set.
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
Parameters currently recognised by built-in signature algorithms are the
same as those for built-in digest algorithms.
See
.Xr provider-digest 7
for further information.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_md_ctx_params 3
and
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_md_ctx_params 3
get a constant
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array that describes the gettable and settable digest parameters, i.e.
parameters that can be used with
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_get_md_ctx_params 3
and
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_set_md_ctx_params 3
respectively.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_newctx
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_dupctx
should return the newly created provider side signature context, or NULL
on failure.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_ctx_params ,
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_ctx_params ,
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_gettable_md_ctx_params 3
and
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_settable_md_ctx_params 3 ,
return the gettable or settable parameters in a constant
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array.
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_signature_query_key_types 3
should return a NULL-terminated array of strings.
.Pp
All verification functions should return 1 for success, 0 for a
non-matching signature, and a negative value for operation failure.
.Pp
All other functions should return 1 for success and 0 or a negative
value for failure.
